WHY — a STATE signal against an EVENT-shaped scheduler
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  BTCUSD   2,934 flagged bars of 3,124 with a defined window (0 exactly-zero returns)
           1 contiguous flag runs, longest 2,928 bars; 441 sign flips
           -> 1 scheduled entries (one per run, at its first bar)
  ETHUSDT  1,260 flagged bars of 1,450 with a defined window (0 exactly-zero returns)
           1 contiguous flag runs, longest 1,254 bars; 209 sign flips
           -> 1 scheduled entries (one per run, at its first bar)
  SOLUSDT  1,258 flagged bars of 1,450 with a defined window (2 exactly-zero returns)
           3 contiguous flag runs, longest 505 bars; 201 sign flips
           -> 3 scheduled entries (one per run, at its first bar)

Every prior family here fires on an EVENT and its flags are sparse.
This one is a STATE: the sign of a 10-bar return is defined on
nearly every bar, so the flags form few long runs. simulate_schedule
takes AT MOST ONE entry per contiguous run (slice 17 — the rule that
makes the rotation null valid), so what governs the sample size is
how often the sign FLIPS, not how many bars are flagged.

